C = [
("h1", "12. The Offence of the Cross"),
("p", "Claim: Paul does not only say what the Cross did. He says how it was received. It was a stumblingblock to some, foolishness to others, the power of God to the called, and a cause of persecution for those who preached it. The Cross also had enemies who walked as its enemies, and Paul tells it weeping. This chapter reads those passages together, because they show something about the Cross that the passages of doctrine do not: what it cost to preach it, and what the preaching of it was set against."),
("sub", "12.1 A stumblingblock and foolishness (1 Corinthians 1:18–25)"),
("p", "The passage has been read in chapter 2 for its place in the gospel. It is read here for its account of how the preaching was received. “For the preaching of the cross is to them that perish foolishness; but unto us which are saved it is the power of God” (1 Corinthians 1:18). “For the Jews require a sign, and the Greeks seek after wisdom: But we preach Christ crucified, unto the Jews a stumblingblock, and unto the Greeks foolishness” (1 Corinthians 1:22, 23). “Because the foolishness of God is wiser than men; and the weakness of God is stronger than men” (1 Corinthians 1:25)."),
("p", "Four receptions are named: foolishness to those who perish, a stumblingblock to the Jews who require a sign, foolishness to the Greeks who seek wisdom, and power and wisdom to the called. The passage does not say that Paul altered the preaching to suit the hearers. It says the same preaching met different expectations. The Jews required a sign and were given a crucified Christ. The Greeks sought wisdom and were given a crucified Christ. The preaching is audience-specific in how it was received, not in what it was."),
("sub", "12.2 “The offence of the cross” (Galatians 5:11; 6:12)"),
("p", "“And I, brethren, if I yet preach circumcision, why do I yet suffer persecution? then is the offence of the cross ceased” (Galatians 5:11). “As many as desire to make a fair shew in the flesh, they constrain you to be circumcised; only lest they should suffer persecution for the cross of Christ” (Galatians 6:12). The two verses present the same contrast from both sides. Paul is persecuted because he does not preach circumcision, and the offence of the cross would cease if he did. The others constrain the Galatians to be circumcised so that they themselves may not suffer persecution for the cross of Christ."),
("ctbl", "What Galatians 5:11 and 6:12 say and what they leave unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“if I yet preach circumcision, why do I yet suffer persecution?”", "Paul’s persecution is evidence that he does not preach circumcision", "Who the persecutors were; the verse does not say"),
    ("“then is the offence of the cross ceased”", "If circumcision were preached, the offence of the cross would cease", "What exactly the offence is; the verse names it as an offence"),
    ("“lest they should suffer persecution for the cross of Christ”", "Those who constrain circumcision do so to avoid persecution for the cross", "Whether this is their only motive; Paul gives it as one"),
  ], [0.32, 0.36, 0.32]),
("p", "The passage says that the Cross offends because it removes the claim of the flesh. If circumcision is preached, the cross is made of no offence. This is the sense in which Paul says in Galatians 2:21 that if righteousness came by the law, Christ is dead in vain. The Cross is incompatible with a righteousness by works, and those who want to keep the works must remove the offence or suffer for it."),
("sub", "12.3 “Crucified through weakness” (2 Corinthians 13:4)"),
("p", "“For though he was crucified through weakness, yet he liveth by the power of God. For we also are weak in him, but we shall live with him by the power of God toward you” (2 Corinthians 13:4). The verse contrasts weakness and power. Christ was crucified through weakness and lives by the power of God, and Paul and his companions are weak in him and shall live with him by the power of God. The Cross is here a pattern for the apostle’s own weakness, which his opponents counted against him. The sentence is a defence of Paul’s ministry as well as a statement about Christ."),
("sub", "12.4 “The enemies of the cross of Christ” (Philippians 3:18, 19)"),
("p", "“(For many walk, of whom I have told you often, and now tell you even weeping, that they are the enemies of the cross of Christ: Whose end is destruction, whose God is their belly, and whose glory is in their shame, who mind earthly things.)” (Philippians 3:18, 19). The phrase is “the enemies of the cross of Christ.” Paul does not say what they teach. He says how they walk, what their end is, what their god is, and what they mind. The Cross is the point at which he marks them. It is the test of a walk."),
("tbl", "How the Cross was received, in Paul’s letters.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("1Cor 1:18", "“the preaching of the cross is to them that perish foolishness”", "To those who perish"),
    ("1Cor 1:23", "“unto the Jews a stumblingblock, and unto the Greeks foolishness”", "To the Jews and the Greeks"),
    ("1Cor 1:24", "“unto them which are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God”", "To the called"),
    ("Gal 5:11", "“then is the offence of the cross ceased”", "Offence, with persecution"),
    ("Gal 6:12", "“lest they should suffer persecution for the cross of Christ”", "Persecution avoided"),
    ("2Cor 13:4", "“he was crucified through weakness, yet he liveth by the power of God”", "Weakness and power"),
    ("Phil 3:18", "“the enemies of the cross of Christ”", "A walk marked by enmity"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "12.5 What the chapter shows"),
("p", "The reception of the Cross is part of what Paul says about it. It is foolishness, an offence, a weakness, and a mark of enemies; and to the called it is power and wisdom. The volume has read the passages of doctrine as complete in themselves, and this chapter adds that they were written in a setting where the Cross was disputed. That setting explains why Paul answers each need with a different figure. A letter that must answer an attempt to add circumcision uses the curse and the offence. A letter that must answer an attempt to add ordinances uses the record and the powers. A letter that must answer the question of God’s righteousness uses the propitiation. The figures are chosen for the fight."),
]
